  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 PM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nA potent 500 mb shortwave trough over Hudson Bay and Ontario\nProvince was digging southward into the western Great Lakes \nbasin and southwest into KS. An associated cold front was over\nnw IL into central MO and will track se across central IL into\nthis evening and through southeast IL by early overnight.\nOvercast low clouds blanket central IL from I-70 north while\nstill mostly sunny skies southeast of I-70 early this afternoon. \nRadar mosaic and surface obs show patchy light rain showers and\ndrizzle nw of the IL river and extending into southeast parts of\nWI/IA and pivoting se toward central IL. As the cold front\nsweeps se through the IL river valley rest of this afternoon and\nand into southeast IL by early overnight, will see low clouds\nincrease in southeast IL rest of today, while chances of light\nrain showers/drizzle spread se as well over the CWA. Rainfall\nmeasurements will be very light if they occur. Breezy WSW winds\ngusting 15-25 mph ahead of cold front to turn NW behind the\nfront and bring in colder air. Lows overnight mostly in the\nlower 40s, with some upper 30s northern CWA and near 45F from\nhighway 50 south in southeast IL. \n\nA 545-550 decameter 500 mb low moving from Ontario into\nsoutheast lower MI Fri afternoon, will keep its light\nprecipitation ne of central IL while low clouds decrease from\nthe north during the day Friday as skies become partly to mostly\nsunny. Cool highs in the upper 40s north of I-72 and lower 50s\nfrom I-72 south. Breezy NNW winds will add a fall chill to the\nair. A 1025 mb Canadian high pressure ridge to settle southeast\ninto the upper Midwest Friday and into MO and ridging over IL\nduring Friday night and Saturday. Winds to lighten up during\nFriday evening with mostly clear skies and dry continental air\nmass to bring coldest night of the season. Late-season \ngardening interests should prepare for widespread frost/freeze \npotential Friday night and Saturday night. A widespread freeze \nlikely over much of CWA by dawn Saturday with lows 28-32F. Still\ncool Saturday with mostly sunny skies, highs in the lower 50s \n(near 55 at Lawrenceville). We should have lighter nw winds \nthan today and Friday with high pressure ridge nearby. Another \nfrosty night Saturday night with lows in the lower 30s. \n\nSurface high pressure ridge shifts south/se of central IL during\nSunday with WSW return flow starting a warming trends. Highs\nSunday around 60F to lower 60s with a fair amount of sunshine\nand not too strong of winds so a pretty nice fall day for\noutdoor activities. Ensemble consensus (GEFS/EPS) shows strong \nagreement on a fairly quiet fall weather pattern over IL during\nduring much of next week, with temperatures trending above\nnormal with highs in the low to mid 60s. A blocking regime\npattern over withe Gulf moisture cutoff from returning to IL and\ndisturbances periodically passing well ne of central IL. \n\nThe Climate Prediction Center (CPC) 8-14 Day Outlook for Nov\n12-18th has a 65-75% chance of above normal temperatures over\ncentral and southeast IL and 33-40% chance of below normal\nprecipitation.",
